From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 01:40:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] skw: decide what deserves a wakeup instead of waking for
 everything

The chip pulls host-wake for anything it has to deliver and the host cannot see
what that is until it is already awake, so on a board whose only input is a BLE
remote every piece of link maintenance ends the suspend. Filtering needs a
moment between the interrupt and the wakeup, and only suspend-to-idle has one:
the CPU services interrupts throughout, and s2idle_loop() exits on
pm_wakeup_pending() rather than on the interrupt itself.

Four things stand in the way, and this takes them in order.

The chip interrupt is armed with enable_irq_wake(), so suspend_device_irq()
marks it IRQD_WAKEUP_ARMED and irq_pm_check_wakeup() takes it instead of the
driver - the handler never runs. Under s2idle_wake_filter it is requested with
IRQF_NO_SUSPEND instead, which is the opposite bargain: the handler runs while
asleep and wakes nothing. The two must never both be set, so with this on
Bluetooth cannot wake a deep suspend at all, and the mode is read-only after
load for that reason.

Reading the packet needs the SDIO host, and dw_mmc-rockchip gates both its
clocks from a system-suspend callback that runs long before the sleep starts.
pm_runtime_force_resume() in suspend_late brings them back once the MMC driver
has finished suspending, and pm_runtime_force_suspend() in resume_early hands
the device back in the state the PM core left it - its own resume callback is
pm_runtime_force_resume(), which only balances against a matching force_suspend.

Completions come from the MMC controller's own interrupt, which
suspend_device_irqs() disables for the whole sleep. It was requested by another
driver without IRQF_SHARED, so a second action cannot be attached, and
__enable_irq() refuses outright on a suspended line. Incrementing its
no_suspend_depth is what is left, reached through irq_get_irq_data() because
irq_to_desc() is exported only under CONFIG_KVM_BOOK3S_64_HV_MODULE. The line is kept masked from the moment the
clocks are gated until they are back, because dw_mci_interrupt() reads
controller registers.

skw_resume_check() waits on a resume_flag that only skw_sdio_resume() sets, so
every transfer helper would stall for 35 s. It returns at once while the filter
is active.

A remote whose link drops answers with directed advertising within seconds, and
that is the same PDU a keypress sends - what separates them is that the reconnect
hunt follows suspend entry and a keypress does not. wake_grace_ms ignores
advertising reports for the first ten seconds of a suspend; data on a live link
always wakes.

That leaves btseekwave_rx_complete() holding the packet with the system still
asleep, which is the whole point: ACL data is a HID report from a connected
remote and an advertising report is a keypress after the link dropped, so those
call pm_system_wakeup(); a payload timeout expiring, a disconnect or a
completed-packet count reaches the stack as normal and simply does not count as
a wakeup. Nothing is masked, so the host's connection state stays consistent -
the difference from masking Disconnect Complete, which left the box unwakeable
for 19.6 h because the host re-enables accept-list scanning in response to that
event.

PROVEN AND UNSAFE - do not ship. Measured on an H96 Max 3518D: six chip
interrupts taken while asleep, read over SDIO and classified, Disconnect
Complete refused a wake and an Extended Advertising Report took one. The
mechanism is not in doubt.

What blocks it is the platform. In real use the box had trouble entering
suspend, trouble leaving it, and reset itself unprompted. The obvious suspect
was this patch, and a control run cleared it: with the module unloaded, a plain
s2idle suspend still logs "rockchip-pm rockchip-suspend: Runtime PM usage count
underflow!". rockchip_pm_config.c computes its state as
mem_sleep_current - PM_SUSPEND_MEM into an unsigned enum, so under
suspend-to-idle pm_config_prepare() takes its out-of-range early return - the
matching pm_runtime_resume_and_get() never runs while pm_config_complete()
still puts. That warning is cosmetic - the skipped SMC configuration only
matters for a suspend ATF performs, and s2idle never reaches
suspend_ops->enter() - so it explains nothing user-visible, and the control that
found it ran at pm_test=platform, which returns before s2idle_loop() and so
never entered the idle path.

The instability is therefore unattributed. The two liberties this patch takes -
driving another driver's device through
pm_runtime_force_resume()/pm_runtime_force_suspend(), and incrementing an
interrupt descriptor's no_suspend_depth behind the interrupt core's back -
remain the leading suspects. Reverted the same day.

The trace is in the worklog; the reasoning behind each obstacle, and where that
reasoning fails, is in s2idle-feasibility.md. Needs mem_sleep=s2idle and
skw_sdio_lite.s2idle_wake_filter=1. Supersedes 0008, which had the first
obstacle wrong; the two must not both be applied. Wi-Fi rides the same
transport, so check it before concluding anything about Bluetooth.

Signed-off-by: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio.h      |   2 +
 drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_main.c | 101 +++++++++++++++++++-
 drivers/swtbt4l/skw_btdriver.c                     |  58 ++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 159 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio.h	2026-09-13 01:18:21
+++ b/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio.h	2026-09-13 01:18:38
@@ -197,6 +197,8 @@
 	int multi_sdio_drivers;
 	u8 cp_downloaded_flag;
 	bool is_host_nonremovable;	/* back Host capabilities */
+	int s2idle_filter;
+	int host_ctrl_irq;
 };
 
 struct debug_vars {
--- a/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_main.c	2026-09-13 01:18:21
+++ b/drivers/seekwaveplatform_lite/sdio/skw_sdio_main.c	2026-09-13 10:40:47
@@ -26,6 +26,9 @@
 #include <linux/of_device.h>
 #include <linux/of_gpio.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
+#include <linux/irqdesc.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>
 #include <linux/mmc/card.h>
 #include <linux/mmc/core.h>
 #include <linux/mmc/host.h>
@@ -49,6 +52,11 @@
 module_param(bind_device, int, S_IRUGO);
 module_param(test_debug, uint, S_IRUGO);
 module_param(skw_use_sdma, int, S_IRUGO);
+
+static bool s2idle_wake_filter;
+module_param(s2idle_wake_filter, bool, S_IRUGO);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(s2idle_wake_filter,
+	"Keep the chip interrupt and the SDIO host running through a suspend-to-idle, so the Bluetooth driver can read what arrived and decide whether it deserves a wakeup. Needs mem_sleep=s2idle; with this set Bluetooth cannot wake a deep suspend at all. Default off.");
 
 #ifndef MMC_CAP2_SDIO_IRQ_NOTHREAD
 #define MMC_CAP2_SDIO_IRQ_NOTHREAD (1 << 17)
@@ -144,6 +152,12 @@
 {
 	struct skw_sdio_data_t *skw_sdio = skw_sdio_get_data();
 	unsigned int timeout;
+
+	/* Nothing is going to set resume_flag while the system is still asleep, and under the
+	 * s2idle filter the transfer this guards is the whole point of being awake.
+	 */
+	if (skw_sdio->s2idle_filter)
+		return;
 
 	timeout = 0;
 	while((!atomic_read(&skw_sdio->resume_flag)) && (timeout++ < 20000))
@@ -704,8 +718,19 @@
 	skw_sdio_info("gpio_In:%d,gpio_out:%d irq %d\n",skw_sdio->gpio_in,
 		skw_sdio->gpio_out, skw_sdio->irq_num);
 	if (skw_sdio->irq_num) {
+		unsigned long flags = skw_sdio->irq_trigger_type | IRQF_ONESHOT;
+
+		/* A wake-armed interrupt is taken by irq_pm_check_wakeup() instead of by us: it
+		 * disables the line and wakes the system without the handler ever running, so
+		 * there is no point at which anything could judge what arrived. IRQF_NO_SUSPEND
+		 * is the opposite bargain - the handler runs while asleep and wakes nothing - and
+		 * the two must never be set on one interrupt.
+		 */
+		if (s2idle_wake_filter)
+			flags |= IRQF_NO_SUSPEND;
+
 		ret = request_irq(skw_sdio->irq_num, skw_gpio_irq_handler,
-				skw_sdio->irq_trigger_type | IRQF_ONESHOT, "skw-gpio-irq", NULL);
+				flags, "skw-gpio-irq", NULL);
 		if (ret != 0) {
 			free_irq(skw_sdio->irq_num, NULL);
 			skw_sdio_err(" request gpio irq fail ret=%d\n", ret);
@@ -716,7 +741,8 @@
 					skw_sdio->irq_num, skw_sdio->device_active);
 		}
 	}
-	enable_irq_wake(skw_sdio->irq_num);
+	if (!s2idle_wake_filter)
+		enable_irq_wake(skw_sdio->irq_num);
 	skw_sdio_rx_up(skw_sdio);
 	return ret;
 }
@@ -809,6 +835,52 @@
 }
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_PM_SLEEP
+static struct device *skw_sdio_host_dev(struct skw_sdio_data_t *skw_sdio)
+{
+	struct sdio_func *func = skw_sdio->sdio_func[FUNC_1];
+
+	if (!func || !func->card || !func->card->host)
+		return NULL;
+	return mmc_dev(func->card->host);
+}
+
+static int skw_sdio_host_ctrl_irq(struct skw_sdio_data_t *skw_sdio)
+{
+	struct device *dev = skw_sdio_host_dev(skw_sdio);
+
+	if (!dev || !dev_is_platform(dev))
+		return 0;
+	return platform_get_irq(to_platform_device(dev), 0);
+}
+
+/* suspend_device_irq() consults no_suspend_depth before anything else and leaves the line alone
+ * when it is set, which is the only way to keep an interrupt live through a suspend-to-idle
+ * without also arming it as a wake source - and arming it is exactly what we are trying to avoid.
+ * The flag belongs at request_irq() time, but the controller interrupt is the MMC driver's.
+ * irq_to_desc() is not exported to modules; irq_get_irq_data() is, and the rest is a header inline.
+ */
+static void skw_sdio_host_ctrl_irq_exempt(struct skw_sdio_data_t *skw_sdio, bool exempt)
+{
+	struct irq_data *data = irq_get_irq_data(skw_sdio->host_ctrl_irq);
+	struct irq_desc *desc = data ? irq_data_to_desc(data) : NULL;
+
+	if (!desc)
+		return;
+	if (exempt)
+		desc->no_suspend_depth++;
+	else
+		desc->no_suspend_depth--;
+}
+
+static bool skw_sdio_s2idle_pending(void)
+{
+#ifdef CONFIG_SUSPEND
+	return pm_suspend_target_state == PM_SUSPEND_TO_IDLE;
+#else
+	return false;
+#endif
+}
+
 int skw_sdio_set_suspend_indication(void)
 {
 	int ret = 0;
@@ -890,9 +962,57 @@
 	 */
 	if (!ret)
 		skw_sdio->suspend_wake_unlock_enable = 1;
+
+	/* Masked rather than left live: the MMC driver gates the SDIO clocks in this same phase,
+	 * just after us, and its handler reads controller registers.
+	 */
+	if (!ret && s2idle_wake_filter && skw_sdio_s2idle_pending()) {
+		if (!skw_sdio->host_ctrl_irq)
+			skw_sdio->host_ctrl_irq = skw_sdio_host_ctrl_irq(skw_sdio);
+		if (skw_sdio->host_ctrl_irq > 0) {
+			disable_irq(skw_sdio->host_ctrl_irq);
+			skw_sdio_host_ctrl_irq_exempt(skw_sdio, true);
+			skw_sdio->s2idle_filter = 1;
+		} else {
+			skw_sdio_warn("no MMC controller irq, s2idle filter off\n");
+		}
+	}
 	return ret;
+}
+
+/* Runs after the MMC driver has suspended, which is the earliest the SDIO host can be brought
+ * back up and the latest anything can still run before the system idles.
+ */
+static int skw_sdio_suspend_late(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct skw_sdio_data_t *skw_sdio = skw_sdio_get_data();
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!skw_sdio->s2idle_filter)
+		return 0;
+
+	ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(skw_sdio_host_dev(skw_sdio));
+	if (ret)
+		skw_sdio_err("SDIO host stays down, nothing can be read while asleep: %d\n", ret);
+	enable_irq(skw_sdio->host_ctrl_irq);
+	return 0;
 }
 
+/* Hand the MMC device back in the state the PM core suspended it in - its own resume callback is
+ * pm_runtime_force_resume(), and that only balances against a matching force_suspend().
+ */
+static int skw_sdio_resume_early(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct skw_sdio_data_t *skw_sdio = skw_sdio_get_data();
+
+	if (!skw_sdio->s2idle_filter)
+		return 0;
+
+	disable_irq(skw_sdio->host_ctrl_irq);
+	pm_runtime_force_suspend(skw_sdio_host_dev(skw_sdio));
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int skw_sdio_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct sdio_func *func = container_of(dev, struct sdio_func, dev);
@@ -903,6 +1023,11 @@
 #if defined(SKW_BOOT_DEBUG)
 	skw_dloader(2);
 #endif
+	if (skw_sdio->s2idle_filter) {
+		skw_sdio->s2idle_filter = 0;
+		enable_irq(skw_sdio->host_ctrl_irq);
+		skw_sdio_host_ctrl_irq_exempt(skw_sdio, false);
+	}
 	skw_sdio_set_resume_indication();
 	skw_sdio->suspend_wake_unlock_enable =0;
 	if (SKW_CARD_ONLINE(skw_sdio))
@@ -1179,6 +1304,7 @@
 
 static const struct dev_pm_ops skw_sdio_pm_ops = {
 	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(skw_sdio_suspend, skw_sdio_resume)
+	SET_LATE_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(skw_sdio_suspend_late, skw_sdio_resume_early)
 };
 
 static const struct sdio_device_id skw_sdio_ids[] = {
--- a/drivers/swtbt4l/skw_btdriver.c	2026-09-13 01:18:21
+++ b/drivers/swtbt4l/skw_btdriver.c	2026-09-13 11:54:34
@@ -116,6 +116,20 @@
     "Keep the BLE link across system suspend, so a paired remote that answers the suspend-time "
     "disconnect with directed advertising cannot wake the host straight back up. Default off.");
 
+/* A remote whose link drops answers with directed advertising within a few seconds, and that is
+ * indistinguishable from a keypress - both are an advertising report from a bonded device. What
+ * separates them is when they arrive: the reconnect hunt follows suspend entry, a keypress does
+ * not. Waking on an advert only once the hunt is over costs a press if someone reaches for the
+ * remote inside the window, and buys a suspend that survives its own teardown.
+ */
+static unsigned int wake_grace_ms = 10000;
+module_param(wake_grace_ms, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(wake_grace_ms,
+    "Ignore advertising reports for this long after suspend entry, so a reconnecting remote does "
+    "not wake the host straight back up. Data on a live link always wakes. 0 disables.");
+
+static unsigned long skwbt_suspend_started;
+
 #if LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(5, 16, 0)
 static struct hci_dev *skwbt_pm_hdev;
 
@@ -132,9 +146,14 @@
     struct hci_dev *hdev = skwbt_pm_hdev;
     struct sk_buff *skb;
 
-    if (action != PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE || !hdev)
+    if (action != PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE)
         return NOTIFY_DONE;
 
+    skwbt_suspend_started = jiffies;
+
+    if (!hdev)
+        return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
     skb = hci_cmd_sync(hdev, HCI_OP_SET_EVENT_MASK_PAGE_2,
                        sizeof(skwbt_evt_mask_page2_off), skwbt_evt_mask_page2_off,
                        HCI_CMD_TIMEOUT);
@@ -338,6 +357,35 @@
 #endif
 
     return 0;
+}
+
+/* The chip pulls host-wake for anything it has to deliver, so this is the first and only place
+ * where input can be told from link maintenance. ACL data is a HID report from a connected remote;
+ * an advertising report is a keypress arriving after the link has dropped. A payload timeout
+ * expiring, a disconnect or a completed-packet count still reaches the stack - none of them is a
+ * reason to end the sleep.
+ */
+static bool skwbt_advert_is_input(void)
+{
+    if(!wake_grace_ms)
+        return true;
+
+    return time_after(jiffies, skwbt_suspend_started + msecs_to_jiffies(wake_grace_ms));
+}
+
+static bool skwbt_wake_worthy(const u8 *buf, int size)
+{
+    if(size < 2)
+        return false;
+
+    if(HCI_ACLDATA_PKT == buf[0])
+        return true;
+
+    if((HCI_EVENT_PKT == buf[0]) && (HCI_EV_LE_META == buf[1]) && (size >= 4))
+        return ((HCI_EV_LE_ADVERTISING_REPORT == buf[3]) || (HCI_EV_LE_EXT_ADV_REPORT == buf[3])) &&
+               skwbt_advert_is_input();
+
+    return false;
 }
 
 int btseekwave_rx_complete(int portno, struct scatterlist *priv, int size, void *buf)
@@ -369,6 +417,14 @@
         return 0;
     }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_SUSPEND
+    /* Restricted to a suspend-to-idle in progress. Awake this would only arm an abort for whatever
+     * suspend comes next, and on the way into a deep suspend the wake was already decided by the
+     * interrupt core, long before the packet reached us.
+     */
+    if((PM_SUSPEND_TO_IDLE == pm_suspend_target_state) && skwbt_wake_worthy((const u8 *)buf, size))
+        pm_system_wakeup();
+#endif
 
     if((HCI_EVENT_PKT == pkt_type) || (HCI_ACLDATA_PKT == pkt_type) || (HCI_SCODATA_PKT == pkt_type))
     {
